{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChDFYLSe6FzB123C74EdA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I concluded that `ublk_ctrl_start_recovery` in `samples/s209.c` is **not vulnerable**, and wrote that to `results/s209.json` with `cwe` set to null and an empty list of vulnerable statements.\n\n- **Locking:** the function holds `ub->mutex` for its whole run, so the state checks and the reset can't race with other control commands.\n- **Queues never set up:** the `!ub->nr_queues_ready` check (line 11) blocks a recovery request on a device whose queues were never made ready. Without that check, `ublk_queue_reinit` could run on queues that don't exist yet. I believe that missing check was the bug the kernel fixed in this function, so this looks like the patched version. That's from my memory of kernel history; I didn't check any source outside the file.\n- **Device state:** recovery only proceeds when the old daemon has closed the device (`UB_STATE_OPEN` is clear) and the device is `UBLK_S_DEV_QUIESCED`. Anything else returns `-EBUSY`.\n- **Reset:** clearing `ub->mm`, `nr_queues_ready`, `nr_privileged_daemon` and the completion happens only after all those checks pass.\n\nI couldn't confirm what `ublk_queue_reinit` or `ublk_get_queue` do, because the task limited me to this one file."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1038,"cache_read_input_tokens":22683,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1038},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8ce7e34-491e-4d9e-bb1e-c10969f2e670","uuid":"c942c75e-bc18-41c5-bc62-6f113466f392","timestamp":"2026-11-20T00:42:14.153Z","request_id":"req_011ChDFYLHieGb1sJFpZACUm"}
